\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The trace ratio on its unit-denominator domain}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{L:M\to M} be an endomorphism of a finite free module
over a commutative unital ring #{k}. On the domain
#{\operatorname{tr}_M(L)\in k^\times}, define the
\newvocab{trace ratio}
##{R(L)=\operatorname{tr}_M(L^2)\bigl(\operatorname{tr}_M(L)\bigr)^{-2},}
using [intrinsic trace](fdip-003G). If #{P:M\to M'} is an
isomorphism, #{c\in k^\times}, and #{L'=cPLP^{-1}}, then
#{\operatorname{tr}_{M'}(L')} is a unit and #{R(L')=R(L)}.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{a=\operatorname{tr}_M(L)}. Linearity, conjugacy and
#{(L')^2=c^2PL^2P^{-1}} give
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}_{M'}(L')&=ca,\\
\operatorname{tr}_{M'}((L')^2)&=c^2\operatorname{tr}_M(L^2).
\end{aligned}}
Both #{c} and #{a} are units, so #{ca} is a unit with inverse
#{a^{-1}c^{-1}}. Substitution cancels #{c^2} from the ratio.}
}

\p{Over #{\mathbb R}, a nonzero self-adjoint positive-semidefinite
operator on a finite-dimensional positive inner-product space has
positive trace. Indeed an [orthonormal eigenbasis](fdip-002R) gives
eigenvalues #{\lambda_i\geq0}; if all were zero the operator would
be zero. Hence
#{\operatorname{tr}L=\sum_i\lambda_i>0}.
This verifies the denominator for a nonzero positive-weight graph
Laplacian. The spectral and trace formulas are
\citet{Appendix A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex}.
The [exceptional examples](fdip-003I) show why nonzero alone is
insufficient over other rings or without positivity.}

\p{For nonzero real positive-semidefinite Laplacians with their
counting pairings, write #{L\approx L'} when
#{L'=cPLP^{-1}} for a positive scalar and a vertex permutation.
Identity uses #{(1,I)}; inverse uses #{(c^{-1},P^{-1})};
composition uses the products of the scalars and permutations.
This is an equivalence relation with invariant #{R}.
It forgets the mass, normalized state and retained partition of an
[enriched background](fdip-000G). It also differs from arbitrary
congruence of bilinear-form arrays: the ratio compares the associated
endomorphisms. It is an invariant, not a classification assertion.}

\p{The ring calculation is a direct consequence of \ref{fdip-003G},
whose trace convention is
\citet{Exercise 22.6, Tag 02DU}{stacks2017stacks}.
Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.trace_smul} supplies the coordinate scalar
identity; the unit domain and cancellation above are additional
hypotheses and deductions. The [cycle comparison](fdip-000I) computes
the invariant and the associated normalized observables explicitly.}
